      <h1>Photo vault app lost my photos on Android: what to do and how to avoid it</h1>
      <p>
        Searching “photo vault lost my photos” is usually a panic moment: the app opens, the PIN
        works, and the library is empty — or you moved to a new phone and nothing came back. This
        guide is a calm checklist. It is not a guarantee you will recover every file. It cites
        public vendor help pages and third-party review samples so you can verify claims yourself.
      </p>

      <h2>Why vault apps “lose” photos so often</h2>
      <p>Most reports cluster around a few mechanical causes, not mysterious deletion:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Local-only data wiped on uninstall.</strong> If items never finished a cloud or
          file backup, reinstalling the app starts from an empty store.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloud caps.</strong> Some products only back up a limited number of items on the
          free plan. Unsynced items stay on that one handset.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cleaner / “boost” apps.</strong> Aggressive cleaners may delete app data folders
          that look like cache.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Trash or recovery behind a paywall.</strong> Files may still exist in an in-app
          Trash that requires a paid plan to restore.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Wrong account or PIN reset without a backup.</strong> Account-based recovery can
          reopen an empty cloud, not the files that never left the old phone.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What major apps say publicly (cited)</h2>
      <h3>Keepsafe</h3>
      <p>
        Keepsafe’s help center states that Photo Vault encrypts files with 256-bit AES at rest and
        that Basic (free) accounts back up the <strong>first 200 items</strong>; paid Premium raises
        the cloud cap (Keepsafe documents up to 10,000 for Premium). The same article warns that if
        you lose a device <em>without</em> backup, photos and videos are gone forever. See
        <a href="https://support.getkeepsafe.com/hc/en-us/articles/360035454311-How-does-Keepsafe-protect-my-data"
          >How does Keepsafe protect my data?</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        Keepsafe also states it is <strong>not designed as a completely zero-knowledge app</strong>
        — employees can access content with user consent for support, when required by law, or when
        investigating Terms/Privacy concerns (
        <a
          href="https://support.getkeepsafe.com/hc/en-us/articles/360035082512-Can-Keepsafe-see-any-of-my-content"
          >Can Keepsafe see any of my content?</a
        >). Separately, recovering items from Trash is documented as a
        <strong>Premium</strong> feature (
        <a href="https://support.getkeepsafe.com/hc/en-us/articles/360035445111-Recovering-items-from-Trash"
          >Recovering items from Trash</a
        >).
      </p>
      <p>
        Independent review samples repeatedly list “lost photos” after updates, new phones or
        reinstalls as a top complaint theme. For example, York Vault’s Keepsafe review (2026)
        reports 111 “lost photos” mentions in a 495-review App Store sample and notes the sample is
        skewed toward negative / “most helpful” sorts — useful as a theme list, <em>not</em> a
        failure rate (
        <a href="https://yorkvault.com/reviews/keepsafe">yorkvault.com/reviews/keepsafe</a>).
        Individual users also describe cloud-backed libraries that support could not restore when
        items were never synced (
        <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/datarecovery/comments/1jbln8z/keepsafe_photo_vault/"
          >r/datarecovery thread, Mar 2025</a
        >).
      </p>

      <h3>NQ Vault and cleaner apps</h3>
      <p>
        NQ Vault’s Google Play presence and user discussions often warn that cleaner apps can wipe
        the app’s data folder (sometimes described around a <code>SystemAndroid</code>-style path).
        Treat “phone cleaner” tools as hostile to any local vault: exclude the vault app from
        cleaners, or avoid them entirely while you store irreplaceable media there. Check the live
        <a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.netqin.ps">NQ Vault Play listing</a>
        and developer replies for the current wording.
      </p>

      <h3>Calculator-style vaults</h3>
      <p>
        Some calculator disguise vaults publish a public master unlock path (for example FishingNet’s
        Calculator vault FAQ on Play has long documented a recovery sequence involving
        <code>11223344</code>). That is convenient for forgotten PINs and terrible against anyone who
        knows the sequence. See the live Play FAQ for
        <a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.hld.anzenbokusucal"
          >Calculator – photo vault</a
        >, and our comparison:
        <a href="/compare/calculator-vault">Vireault vs calculator vault apps</a>.
      </p>

