      <h2>What to do right now (recovery checklist)</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Stop wiping the phone.</strong> Do not factory reset, “clear data” on the vault
          app, or run a storage cleaner until you have exhausted recovery options.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Confirm you are in the right vault / account.</strong> Multi-vault apps and
          account-based products can open an empty container while the full library sits under
          another login or vault.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check in-app Trash / Recently deleted.</strong> If recovery requires Premium, that
          is a product decision — verify on the vendor’s help page before paying, and export anything
          you recover immediately.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check the vendor cloud status.</strong> For Keepsafe-style products, confirm whether
          items finished syncing before the old phone was wiped. Unsynced items will not reappear
          after reinstall.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Search the device for leftover folders.</strong> Some hiders leave a named folder
          under shared storage. File managers or a USB connection may still show encrypted or
          renamed files even when the UI is empty — do not delete unknown folders yet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Contact support with facts, not rage.</strong> App version, Android version,
          whether you uninstalled, whether backup/cloud was enabled, and approximate item count.
          Keep expectations realistic: if the vendor never held a copy, they cannot invent one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Professional data recovery</strong> is a last resort for physical failure, not for
          “I cleared app data.” It is expensive and often fails on encrypted app-private storage.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>How to avoid this next time</h2>
      <h3>1. Own a backup file you can copy</h3>
      <p>
        Prefer vaults that let you export an encrypted archive to Files, Drive, a USB stick or
        another phone — and that let you restore without holding your media hostage. Practice a
        restore drill once before you need it. Vireault’s model is
        <a href="/features/backup-restore">encrypted backup and restore</a> under a recovery code you
        control; there is no vendor cloud that can resurrect a forgotten secret.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Know what “backed up” means on free plans</h3>
      <p>
        If a product only syncs the first N items, treat everything after N as local-only. Export or
        upgrade before a phone swap. Keepsafe’s published Basic cap of 200 items is an example of
        why “I thought everything was in the cloud” fails.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Ban cleaners from vault app data</h3>
      <p>
        Add the vault to every cleaner’s exclusion list, or uninstall cleaners. Local ciphertext is
        still just files to a dumb wiper.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Prefer real cryptography over disguise</h3>
      <p>
        Hiding an album or dressing as a calculator does not authenticate file bytes. Read how the
        app claims to encrypt — key derivation, cipher mode, whether names are encrypted — and what
        it admits it cannot do. Our
        <a href="/security">security page</a> and
        <a href="/guides/secure-photo-vault-android">secure photo vault guide</a> spell out
        Vireault’s Argon2id + AES-256-GCM model and its limits.
      </p>

      <h3>5. Keep gallery / cloud copies until restore is proven</h3>
      <p>
        When migrating, do not delete originals or cancel cloud photo backups until you have opened
        the vault on the new device and verified a sample of files. Optional “delete original after
        import” is convenient only after trust is earned.
      </p>

      <h3>6. Watch paywalls that sit in front of your own files</h3>
      <p>
        Trash recovery or restore tools gated behind Premium are a common complaint theme in public
        reviews. Prefer products that never hold basic unlock or export of <em>your</em> ciphertext
        hostage. Compare Keepsafe-style tradeoffs on
        <a href="/compare/keepsafe">Vireault vs Keepsafe</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Where Vireault fits (honest)</h2>
